## The b in mx + b is eliminated in favor of m(x - a) + y_0

## Tagged by YL


## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of differentiation)
## DBsection(Linear approximation and differentials)
## Date(5/29/2005)
## Institution(UVA)
## Author(Jeff Holt)
## MLT(LinearApprox_EstimateFunction_Root)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus: Early Transcendentals')
## AuthorText1('Stewart')
## EditionText1('5')
## Section1('3.11')
## Problem1('')
## TitleText2('Calculus: Early Transcendentals')
## AuthorText2('Stewart')
## EditionText2('6')
## Section2('3.10')
## Problem2('')
## KEYWORDS('derivatives', 'linear approximation')

DOCUMENT();        # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "PGML.pl",
  "contextFraction.pl",
  "PGcourse.pl"
);

$a1 = random(4,9,1);
$a2 = $a1*$a1;
$d1 = random(.1,.4,.1);
$b1 = $a2+$d1;
$twoa = 2*$a1;
Context("Fraction");
$m1 = Fraction(1/$twoa);
$y1 = $a1;

Context("LimitedNumeric")->flags->set(tolerance => .000001, tolType => "absolute");
Context()->operators->redefine('/');
Context()->parens->redefine('(');

BEGIN_PGML
Use local linearization to approximate [`\sqrt{[$b1]}`] as follows:

Let [`f(x) = \sqrt x`].  The local linearization of [`f`] at [`[$a2]`] is [`l_{[$a2]}(x) =  y_0 + m(x-[$a2])`],
where [`m ={}`][_]{$m1}{10} and [`y_0 ={}`][_]{$a1}{10}.

Using this, we find our approximation [`\sqrt{[$b1]} \approx l_{[$a2]}([$b1]) ={}`][_]{$d1/$twoa + $y1}{20}.

NOTE: For this part, use fractions or give your approximation to at least 6 decimal places.
END_PGML

ENDDOCUMENT();        # This should be the last executable line in the problem.
